    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm looking at some data for a new low-fat mayo formulation and hitting some stability issues in our accelerated aging tests. The pH is quite low (around 3.5) and we're seeing significant phase separation after 48 hours. Can you walk me through the specific role lecithin plays in stabilizing the oil-water interface in these high-acid environments?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lecithin, a phospholipid, acts as a natural emulsifier by having both hydrophilic and lipophilic regions. In your low-pH environment, lecithin molecules rapidly adsorb to the oil-water interface, forming a protective barrier that physically prevents the dispersed oil droplets from coalescing. This strong steric and electrostatic hindrance maintains the stable emulsion structure even when the high acidity challenges the system's stability over time.",
      "source": "target",
