    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, but I'm worried that the steric hindrance might not be enough given the low fat content. Since we have much less oil to provide bulk, do you think the depletion flocculation effect could be more of a factor here, or is it strictly a matter of the lecithin's adsorption density at that pH?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Given the low fat content, depletion flocculation due to the presence of large non-adsorbing polymers in the continuous phase could certainly be a contributing factor. However, the primary mechanism is still the packing efficiency of the lecithin at the interface; if the surface coverage is insufficient, any secondary forces like depletion attraction will easily drive coalescence. You may need to increase the concentration of lecithin to ensure complete and dense interfacial coverage, regardless of depletion effects.",
      "source": "target",
